{"preference":"A","margin":4,"reasons":"The design is a non-randomized internal pilot comparison. The 3-point gap (11 vs 8) cannot support a causal reading because selection into tutoring is uncontrolled. Candidate A keeps the caveat where the number appears (\"not randomized, so the difference may reflect which students were selected\") and in the summary line (\"we cannot yet say the tutoring caused the gains\"). Candidate B drops both. Its summary says only \"early results are encouraging\", and its results paragraph gives the 11 vs 8 comparison with no design information. A board member reading before a budget vote would likely take the gap as a program effect. The evaluator's caveat and the word \"preliminary\" appear only in later sections, so the hedge is far from the claim. For a one-read audience, A is clearly better. A also keeps the original content and adds only the design limitation that the source itself states.","objections":[{"candidate":"B","category":"hedge_removed","quote":"the program reached a lot of students, and early results are encouraging.","claim":"The summary line drops the statement that causation cannot yet be inferred, so a skimming reader gets a favorable conclusion with no limit on it.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"B","category":"design_omitted","quote":"tutored students gained 11 scale-score points on average; students who were not tutored gained 8 points.","claim":"A two-group gain comparison appears with no note that it was non-randomized and that selection could explain the difference. The reader is left to infer a treatment effect. Support would need random assignment or covariate adjustment and an uncertainty estimate.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"A","category":"missing_uncertainty","quote":"tutored students gained 11 scale-score points on average; students who were not tutored gained 8 points.","claim":"Even with the design caveat, no variance, confidence interval or effect size is given for the 3-point difference, and no baseline equivalence is reported. A board member cannot tell whether 3 points is educationally meaningful or distinguishable from noise.","severity":"minor"},{"candidate":"A","category":"cost_framing","quote":"or approximately $688 per student.","claim":"Cost per student is not tied to any effect estimate, so it cannot show cost-effectiveness. The page does not warn against reading it that way.","severity":"minor"}],"allegations":[{"candidate":"A","quote":"approximately $688 per student","claim":"$2.9 million divided by 4,218 students is about $687.5, so $688 is arithmetically consistent. This is worth confirming against the budget source."},{"candidate":"A","quote":"This comes from an internal pilot comparison that was not randomized","claim":"The source facts for the pilot design and the selection mechanism are not shown in the packet. Both candidates could not be checked against the original, so this characterization is unverified."}],"out_of_lane_notes":"Candidate A does not say what action the board should take, such as how to treat the funding request given the preliminary evidence. The task asks that a member can act after one read, and a clear decision-relevant sentence would help. Neither candidate says how many comparison students there were or how the comparison group was formed."}